<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles X Gustav</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1622">1622</a>–<a href="/years/1660">1660</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charles X Gustav was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> in the mid-seventeenth century and a gifted military commander. After a daring winter crossing of the frozen straits between Danish islands, he forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> to accept the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-roskilde">Treaty of Roskilde</a> in 1658. The treaty gave Sweden <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scania">Scania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blekinge">Blekinge</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halland">Halland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bornholm">Bornholm</a> and Norwegian provinces, bringing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire</a> to its greatest extent.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-x-gustav.jpg" alt="Charles X Gustav" width="320" height="398" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sébastien Bourdon · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Charles_X_Gustavus_(S%C3%A9bastien_Bourdon)_-_Nationalmuseum_-_19702.tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Worse followed in 1658, when <b>Charles X Gustav</b> of Sweden marched his army across the frozen Belts and imposed the Treaty of Roskilde, which took Scania, Halland, Blekinge, Bohuslän, Trøndelag and Bornholm; Copenhagen survived a Swedish assault in 1659, and Trøndelag and Bornholm were returned in 1660.</blockquote><a href="/empire/denmark-norway">Denmark-Norway · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1658 <b>Charles X Gustav</b> of Sweden marched his army across the frozen belts.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-denmark">Kingdom of Denmark · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Charles X Gustav</b> crossed the frozen belts to force the Treaty of Roskilde on Denmark in 1658, and Charles XI’s allotment system (indelningsverket) settled soldiers on farms that maintained them.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sweden">Kingdom of Sweden · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>At the Treaty of Roskilde (1658), imposed by <b>Charles X Gustav</b> after his celebrated March across the Belts over the frozen straits, Denmark–Norway ceded Scania, Blekinge, Halland, Bornholm, Bohuslän and Trøndelag, the region of Trondheim; Bornholm and Trøndelag were returned in 1660, but the remaining provinces fixed the…</blockquote><a href="/empire/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>A cadet branch, Palatinate-Kleeburg, gave Sweden three kings, beginning with <b>Charles X Gustav</b> in 1654, and from 1681 to 1718 Zweibrücken itself was ruled by the kings of Sweden.</blockquote><a href="/state/palatinate-zweibrucken">Palatinate-Zweibrücken</a></li></ul></section>
